{
  // ── The BUILD config. `tsconfig.json` stays the TYPECHECK config. ──────────
  //
  // ⚠ Two files, and the split is the whole point. `packages/{shared,redact,
  // flow-edit}` exclude `src/**/*.test.ts` from their single `tsconfig.json`,
  // which keeps `dist/` clean — and also means `pnpm typecheck` (a bare
  // `tsc --noEmit` on that same file) silently stops checking their tests. This
  // package has 53 test files, several of which READ SOURCE OFF DISK and assert
  // on what they find; dropping type coverage on those to tidy the build would
  // trade one silent gap for another.
  //
  // So `build` points here and `typecheck` keeps pointing at `tsconfig.json`.
  // Nothing about what is checked changes; only what is emitted.
  //
  // ── What this fixes ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  //
  // `tsc -p tsconfig.json` was compiling every `*.test.ts` into `dist/`, so:
  //
  //   · the SHIPPED SERVER IMAGE carried 73 compiled test files. The Dockerfile
  //     copies `dist/` wholesale, so test code was in production — including the
  //     suites that read the source tree and the ones that open Postgres.
  //   · a `vitest run` from the REPO ROOT (which has no vitest config, so no
  //     `exclude`) collected `dist/**/*.test.js` as well as `src/**/*.test.ts`
  //     and reported ~20 failures that were only ever the compiled copies
  //     resolving their on-disk paths against `dist/` instead of `src/`. Two
  //     source suites failed alongside them for the same reason — a
  //     grep-the-tree assertion matching its own compiled twin.
  //
  // The per-package command (`pnpm -r test`, what the root `test` script runs)
  // was never affected: `packages/server/vitest.config.ts` scopes itself to
  // `src/`. So this was invisible from the command anyone actually runs.
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "exclude": ["node_modules", "dist", "src/**/*.test.ts"]
}
